{
  "id": "happy-number",
  "questionId": "happy-number",
  "slug": "happy-number",
  "title": "Happy Number",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "cycle detection"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "the sequence of squared-digit sums either reaches 1, or it must eventually repeat a value it has seen before, since there are only finitely many possible sums for numbers of a given size - what does 'eventually repeats' remind you of?",
    "you don't need to store every value you've seen - you can detect a repeat the same way you'd detect a cycle in a linked list",
    "define a 'next' step as replacing a number with the sum of squares of its digits, then run a slow pointer that takes one step at a time and a fast pointer that takes two steps at a time",
    "if the fast pointer ever reaches 1, the number is happy; if the fast and slow pointers ever meet at a value that isn't 1, you're in a loop and the number is not happy"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a hash set to detect repeats when the problem wants O(1) space via cycle detection",
    "forgetting to handle n = 1 as an immediate true",
    "computing the sum of digits instead of the sum of squares of digits"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a positive integer `n`. Repeatedly replace `n` with the sum\nof the squares of its digits.\n\n**Return true if this process eventually reaches 1, and false if it instead enters a loop that never reaches 1.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 1 (already happy, no iterations needed)",
      "n is a single digit that is not 1 (e.g. n = 2, which loops)",
      "n with repeated digits (e.g. 11, 100)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 19",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "19 -> 1^2+9^2=82 -> 8^2+2^2=68 -> 6^2+8^2=100 -> 1^2+0^2+0^2=1"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 2",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "the sequence of squared-digit sums starting from 2 loops forever without hitting 1"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 1",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "n is already 1"
      }
    ],
